A||||7||Shiner||Popping off the doors?? Help a brother out!!||||5||
Z||000000||Shiner||09-26-2000||11:21 PM||||I need to take the doors off my '86 SE and put them on my '87 GT.  For the sake of brevity, I won't go into the whole thing.  So how do I go about doing this?  The stinkin Haynes manual doesn't say anything about removing the doors.  <BR> <BR> Thanks in advance!||||reg||5
Z||000001||SibERThuG||09-26-2000||11:36 PM||||Open door. Now, keeping hold of the door so it dosn't fall or drop, take doorpins and bushings out. If you have power windows, you need to disconnect em'. That should be all. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||HugGRRRFiero||09-26-2000||11:37 PM||||Yep...  That does it<P>Have a friend help you steady the door.<P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">          <BR>          <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by HugGRRRFiero (edited 09-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||09-27-2000||09:07 AM||||that's one way to do it I guess. I'd take the hinges off the A post unless you are replacing the hinge bushings while you do this. You also have to fight with the bloody spring in the bottom hinge.<P>I just did this job to add power windows to my SC. It isn't fun no matter how you do it.<P>To get to the hinges take down the innner fender enough to reach behind it. You also need to take off the plastic trim along the edge of the carpet. GM put one bolt thru the "wrong way" because of a clearance problem. The connectors for the door wires are under the carpet. (Optionally you could take the hinges off the doors. It's a pain either way.)<P>There are extensive notes about the doors in my cave. You'll need them. There are also some adjustments you'll likely need to make to the window glass or it won't work right. (The window adjustment notes are in the next cave update. I'll put them in this thread later. I don't have them here with me) I had to adjust the glass max hieght and the for/aft position.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||09-27-2000||09:24 AM||||I just received my GM Door Hing Spring Removal Tool that I read about in (I believe) Oliver Scholz's page.  It cost about $16.00 US and looks like it will be pretty handy when I have to replace the hinge pin bushings in my 85GT.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-27-2000||03:19 PM||||do you have the part # for that thing?<P>I've done it a few ways. The tool is the best one.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Shiner||09-28-2000||09:48 PM||||Thanks for the input guys.  Both the doors and the hinges are coming off the '86 and going on to the '87, so I'm not sure if I should just pop out the pins or take the hinges off the chassis.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||09-28-2000||11:22 PM||||Theogre,<P>I got the Steck GM Door Hinge Spring Tool from ToolsUSA (800) 451-2425 <A HREF="http://www.toolsusa.com" TARGET=_blank>www.toolsusa.com</A>  look under hand/specialty tools or go directly to the page via the following link:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.toolsusa.com/ToolsUSA/catpg.icl?secid=9&orderidentifier=ID9701716792907350793F3B3C5F&itmcnt=163&begincount=91&endcount=120&curpage=4&numpages=6" TARGET=_blank>http://www.toolsusa.com/ToolsUSA/catpg.icl?secid=9&orderidentifier=ID9701716792907350793F3B3C5F&itmcnt=163&begincount=91&endcount=120&curpage=4&numpages=6</A> <P>Part number (they call it ID number) 4520<P>current cost is $15.22 us<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
